<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Enele Maʻafu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1816–<a href="/years/1881">1881</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Maʻafu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Enele Maʻafu was a Tongan prince who went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a> in the 1840s to look after Tongan interests and built up power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lau-islands">Lau Islands</a> and eastern Fiji. He formed the Tovata confederacy and was the chief rival of Seru Cakobau. He later accepted a place in Cakobau's Kingdom of Fiji and signed the cession of Fiji to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1874.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/enele-maafu.jpg" alt="Enele Maʻafu" width="320" height="437" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Colonel Stewart · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ma%27afu,_the_Tongan_Chief,_photograph_by_late_Colonel_Stewart.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
